{"name":"Sandalwood Beige","hex":"#f2d1b1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sandalwood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various tropical trees of the genus Santalum, native or long naturalized in India, Australia, Hawaii, and many south Pacific islands.","The aromatic heartwood of these trees used in ornamental carving, in the construction of insect-repellent boxes and chests, and as a source of certain perfumes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sandalwood"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sandalwood","blocks":[{"text":"Sandalwood is a class of woods from trees in the genus Santalum. The woods are heavy, yellow, and fine-grained, and, unlike many other aromatic woods, they retain their fragrance for decades. Sandalwood oil is extracted from the woods. Sandalwood is often cited as one of the most expensive woods in the world. Both the wood and the oil produce a distinctive fragrance that has been highly valued for centuries. Consequently, some species of these slow-growing trees have suffered over-harvesting in the past."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Santalum_paniculatum_1326.jpg/330px-Santalum_paniculatum_1326.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandalwood","wikidata":"Q11122366"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige","wikidata":"Q843607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/A_closeup_of_Sandal_saplings.JPG/500px-A_closeup_of_Sandal_saplings.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/A_closeup_of_Sandal_saplings.JPG/1280px-A_closeup_of_Sandal_saplings.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/A_closeup_of_Sandal_saplings.JPG/500px-A_closeup_of_Sandal_saplings.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A closeup of sandal saplings","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_closeup_of_Sandal_saplings.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Santalum_album_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-128.jpg/330px-Santalum_album_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-128.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Santalum_album_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-128.jpg/330px-Santalum_album_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-128.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Santalum album","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASantalum_album_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-128.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Sandal_leaf.jpg/500px-Sandal_leaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org